.TH IFCONFIG 1
.SH NAME
ifconfig \- the network interface in use
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ifconfig
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ifconfig
prints what the network server knows of the interface in use:
.B nic0
(the cable) or
.B wifi
(Wi-Fi), its link (up, down or unknown), the card's MAC address and the
MTU; the address and mask, the gateway and the DNS server (from DHCP); the
packets and bytes received and sent, the number of sockets open; and the
time (seconds since 1970, from the clock chip). It prints
.B no network card
when there is none.
.PP
.B ifconfig
is another name of
.BR fetch (1)
.RB ( "fetch \-i"
is its older name). It only shows: the address comes from DHCP, and the
Wi-Fi network is chosen with
.BR wifi (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if there is no network card or no network server.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR netstat (1),
.BR wifi (1),
.BR fetch (1),
.BR netd (4)
